# Implement explicit import and export

## Scope and existing evidence

Allow deliberate notebook bundle and `.atrament` profile reads or writes at
caller-selected or explicitly host-policy-admitted paths without converting them
into background persistence. Keep paths mentioned only in notebook, source,
diagnostic, clipboard, or model-response text as data rather than Export
authority.

Current export design evidence freezes accepted-revision binding, caller-owned
or
explicit host-policy output intent, explicit path and overwrite behavior,
blocking validation, temporary cleanup, file-commit semantics, output identity,
same-retry lost-receipt recovery, external target drift handling, cancellation,
typed output results, concurrency, and browser/CLI/MCP parity. File adapters and
format-specific execution remain open. Execution is also blocked on a typed
exportable-artifact payload: current backend code has semantic layout preflight
but no renderer or serializer result value whose admitted bytes a file adapter
can commit. Filesystem writes must not guess that missing artifact-ownership
boundary.

The shared derived/output result domain now freezes all 11 first-release core
result classes and their operation applicability across Render, Plan, and
Export.
It remains vocabulary only: no output operation, receipt normalization, retry
recovery, path handling, or file effect is implemented by that domain.

Its effect disposition separately distinguishes current-call read-only
projection, current-call file commit, recovered prior file commit, and known no
new effect without claiming any receipt or retry-store implementation.

A combined operation/result helper now exposes that disposition only for an
applicable Render, Plan, or Export pair, so callers cannot skip applicability
validation and accidentally project an operation-incompatible result.


Already-qualified lifecycle cancellation can now project into those same output
results without guessing execution state: proven pre-boundary cancellation maps
to Cancelled before result or effect; a crossed Render/Plan completion remains
Completed projection; and a crossed Export file commit remains Exported. A
request alone produces no final result. Cancellation signaling, adapter
execution, receipt normalization, and retry storage remain open.

## Explicit path-intent classification evidence

A transport-neutral `atrament_explicit_file_path_intent` domain now
classifies notebook and handwriting-profile imports and exports before any
file effect. It distinguishes caller selection and independently admitted
host policy from untrusted notebook/source, model, clipboard, and diagnostic
text; all content-derived sources are rejected as path intent.

The contract also rejects missing file-name components, disallows overwrite
requests for Import, preserves opaque non-UTF-8 selected paths, and retains
an explicit preserve-or-replace choice for Export. Integration fixtures cover
all 48 source/purpose/overwrite combinations plus invalid and opaque paths.

The classification is not a filesystem capability and does not check the
truthfulness of its claimed source. The future trusted caller or host adapter
must establish that source independently before invoking this contract and
still enforce path policy, symlink safety, serializer provenance, export
preflight, atomic commits, retry identity, and cancellation. No file has
been imported or exported by this work; the task remains open.

Autonomous Export intent now composes that source-classified path with the
existing autonomous goal-source gate. A 24-case fixture proves that model
text cannot authorize Export even if an independent path is selected, and
that an Import-class path cannot be substituted for an Export target. This
combination is still read-only and does not authorize a file effect.

## Read-only composite Export prerequisite evidence

An `atrament_export_prerequisite_projection` application now combines the
existing independent autonomous Export-source gate, exact Import-versus-Export
selected-path purpose, and revision-bound layout preflight. Its outcomes are
`NoExportIntent`, `WrongPathPurpose`, `BlockedLayout`, `IncompleteLayout`, and
`RequiresArtifactAndFileAdmission`: even the last outcome is **not** complete
Export readiness and cannot authorize a file effect. Source and path selection
remain claims that a future trusted transport must independently verify.

The composition is also reachable through the process-owned
`SessionApplication::preflight_explicit_export_prerequisites` method, which
performs the existing layout-revision binding against current accepted state
before projecting anything. Regression fixtures exercise the 72
source/purpose/layout combinations, genuine empty/incomplete layout evidence,
a genuine 6 mm fixed-region overflow, stale revision rejection after an
accepted semantic edit, and fresh-session refusal. Output artifacts, path
policy, serialization, atomic file commits, retries, and cancellation remain
unimplemented. No persistent output is created by this work.

The selected-file classifier now also rejects embedded NUL bytes before any
future file adapter can interpret the path, while retaining exact non-UTF-8
names and refusing directory-marker paths. Raw paths ending in `/.` or
`/..` now also reject before `Path::file_name()` can discard a trailing dot
component and mistake a directory selection for a file. The caller/host
selection provenance rule remains unchanged and no file effect is introduced.

## Explicit portable-profile Import preflight evidence

The new read-only `atrament_profile_import_preflight` application composes
trusted-source-classified `ImportProfile` file intent with canonical stored ZIP
encoding, admitted manifest features, and an exact declared-versus-observed
member-name inventory. Other import or export purposes reject **before** any
archive evidence is considered. Only after all gates pass does it return
borrowed, constructor-sealed evidence from which separately observed entry
lengths and SHA-256 digests may be checked before a future decoder sees bytes.

The Import fixture covers all 16 stored/compressed, extras, and ZIP64
combinations, rejected source/path purposes, unknown required features, missing
archive members, exact entry lookup without percent decoding, and length-before-
digest failure precedence. The caller must still prove the source provenance,
read and parse real archive bytes, establish measurements and hashes, validate
typed section schemas and resource bounds, and explicitly accept the resulting
profile into session state. Preflight success **does not** import a file,
persist anything, or claim an external path capability.

A mandatory, caller-selected Import resource budget now rejects excessive
manifest entry counts, oversized individual declared entries, aggregate declared
bytes, and `u64` sum overflow. The gate also caps observed archive-name counts
**before** allocating inventory membership sets. Tests cover exact bounds,
zero budgets, wrong-purpose precedence, and declared/observed limit failures.
These constraints operate on already-parsed declarations and name evidence:
actual decompressed byte counts, compression expansion, parser allocation,
media decoding and runtime memory ceilings must be enforced separately.

The separate in-memory `atrament_profile_zip_reader` now obtains ordinary
stored-only ZIP member names and byte slices from actual bounded ZIP framing,
including independently checked CRC-32. Its integration regression feeds
those observed names into this task's existing typed `ImportProfile` gate.
Actual filesystem admission, bounded file reading, manifest JSON decoding,
cryptographic digest recomputation, ZIP64, and user-controlled session profile
acceptance are still required before this TODO can close.

The separate profile-entry-sha256 primitive now provides exact SHA-256
computation and byte-length-first verification over admitted in-memory
member bytes. It has no file path, ZIP/JSON parsing, or accepted-profile
mutation. A future Import adapter must still establish genuine caller-owned
source authority, decode and validate the manifest, connect the archive's
exact byte slices to declarations, and explicitly commit an accepted profile.

The stored-ZIP inbound adapter now verifies **every** declared non-manifest
member against its exact bytes and SHA-256 after independently sealed
inventory preflight. It rechecks ZIP member names against that admitted
inventory before hashing, and returns borrowed entry bytes only after every
member succeeds. Cross-archive substitution, valid-CRC digest corruption,
and near-match entry lookup are regression-tested. The typed manifest still
needs to be parsed and bound to this ZIP's own root JSON; no file is opened
and no profile is accepted by this byte-level step.

A separate resource-bounded JSON syntax domain now validates one complete
UTF-8 root object and retains borrowed source bytes without allocating typed
values. The ZIP inbound adapter binds that syntax gate to the actual CRC-
checked `manifest.json` bytes and checks root byte, value and nesting limits.
The gate now rejects duplicate decoded keys in every object, even across
Unicode escapes and surrogate-pair aliases, using bounded fixed-size storage.
It still does not decode a typed manifest, establish the manifest's wire schema,
or authorize Import from any caller-selected filesystem path.

The constructor-sealed root JSON syntax result can now borrow a chosen
root-member value's exact byte slice from the checked ZIP manifest entry.
Lookup is Unicode-scalar exact and cannot turn nested fields or near-match
names into top-level values. The caller still cannot treat this generic byte
view as a typed import or a verified caller-authorized filesystem read.

Nested sealed JSON value views now distinguish six syntactic JSON kinds and
support exact-key object lookup, ordered array traversal and bounded indexed
array selection from one admitted ZIP manifest root. Structural selection is
not yet a typed profile manifest, a declared-entry digest proof, or authorized
filesystem Import; those checks remain mandatory before session mutation.

The JSON syntax evidence can now expose a decoded string scalar iterator and
non-coercing bool/null/plain `u64` helpers from an admitted ZIP root. The typed
manifest parser still must bind every required field to the actual root bytes,
refuse schema mismatches and unknown required features, and preserve optional
wire data before any Import/session effects.

Source-order sealed JSON member enumeration now retains original key spelling
and exact selected values without constructing a typed manifest. The future
codec must decide required versus optional keys and reject incompatible fields
before verified inventory, explicitly selected path and session Import effects.

Read-only sealed manifest JSON views now allow a caller-provided sequence of
object keys and array indices to select one original source-byte value, even
through nested ZIP-root structures. The actual typed manifest parser must
still decide allowed keys, enforce required/unknown field handling, bind
declared SHA-256 entries to this root, and pass explicit path authorization.

Decoded manifest JSON strings can now be copied without heap allocation into
bounded caller-owned storage, atomically refusing inadequate destination space
and non-string values. This does not permit using decoded values as paths,
selecting a source, or accepting a typed profile until the future import codec
and explicit filesystem authority establish those independent checks.

Sealed JSON member names now support bounded decoded UTF-8 copies without
allocating a JSON tree or reinterpreting input as path authority. Future typed
manifest schema recognition and unknown-field preservation still need their
own explicit policy before section decoding or session Import.

The stored-ZIP reader can now pair its own CRC-checked archive index and exact
syntax-admitted root JSON bytes in a sealed borrowed view. A future typed
manifest decoder can receive that association instead of independent arguments.
The typed schema, all declared SHA-256 entry checks, selected path authority,
and session acceptance are still unimplemented and cannot be inferred here.

Incremental SHA-256 evidence now supports future bounded streaming Import
without retaining an entire member as one allocation. The pure chunk verifier
checks declared byte length before comparing SHA-256 and does not open any file,
validate source path authority, or accept a profile. Actual I/O errors and
explicit path/source admission remain responsibilities of future adapters.

A fallible variant of the fixed-memory chunk verifier now preserves upstream
transport errors distinctly from short streams, declared length failures or
SHA-256 mismatches. The verifier consumes only caller-supplied in-memory
`Result` chunks; actual file access, interruption, authorization, cleanup and
profile acceptance remain unimplemented in the adapter layer.

Already SHA-256-verified stored ZIP payloads can now be accessed as borrowed,
nonzero-sized chunks, including distinct present-empty and absent cases.
Future section importers can consume those exact slices without whole-payload
copies; format decoding, source authorization, filesystem I/O and session
acceptance must still be implemented independently.

A bounded inbound reader-verification adapter now accepts only an independently
preopened byte source and a typed entry declaration. It refuses over-budget
declarations before I/O, hashes in fixed 8 KiB space and requires exact EOF
without buffering whole members. Original I/O errors stay distinct from a
short stream, excess bytes and digest mismatch. Trusted file opening, stream
timeouts, path authority and accepted-profile mutation remain future work.

Transport verification currently proves EOF only for the reader view supplied
by the trusted adapter, not necessarily the physical source beneath wrappers.
`Read::take` can hide trailing original bytes, so the eventual selected-file
reader must bind the complete original source scope and length independently.
The verifier explicitly tests and documents this distinction; it cannot serve
as proof of a selected source by itself.

Adversarial `Read` fixtures also reject impossible reported byte counts without
panicking and treat premature zero counts as EOF, not a way to retry hidden
bytes. The selected host transport must still ensure truthful Read semantics.

A separate pure application can produce deterministic stored-only ZIP framing
in memory from already chosen section bytes and an opaque supplied root.
It never serializes a typed manifest or writes a path; the missing canonical
JSON codec, explicit output artifact identity, selected destination authority,
atomic file commit and retry recovery still block genuine profile Export.

The pure stored-ZIP framing application can now preview exact final archive
size through the same failure checks used by its byte writer, without output
allocation or checksum work. This only supports eventual output resource
planning: the future host still needs source authenticity, typed manifest
serialization, explicit file intent and independent atomic commit authority.

Source-sealed ZIP root JSON also has a non-authorizing SHA-256 fingerprint
computed from exact CRC-checked, syntax-admitted root bytes. The fingerprint
is deliberately root-scoped: it does not establish full archive identity,
manifest semantic agreement, file authority or accepted Import state.

The checked archive now exposes a distinct SHA-256 of its complete original
ZIP source in addition to the root-only JSON SHA-256. Changed non-root bytes
alter only the archive-wide fingerprint; neither digest binds a selected file
handle, a trusted destination, a schema-valid manifest or Import acceptance.

Read-only source-backed ZIP framing evidence can now distinguish a readable
legacy-metadata archive from bytes matching the pure stored-ZIP writer. Even
when the complete framing matches, future Import must still admit canonical
JSON, a schema-typed root, declared entry hashes, chosen source authority and
session mutation independently; this indicator performs none of those steps.

The checked ZIP reader can now copy its exact archive path references into
caller-owned bounded storage with atomic too-small rejection. Future Import
inventory preflight can use this view instead of an infallibly allocated `Vec`
when memory policy requires it, but validated path names do not bind a typed
manifest or authorize a filesystem read.

The existing read-only explicit-profile Import preflight fixture now consumes
caller-owned borrowed ZIP paths directly, including its missing-root failure.
No additional observed-name vector is needed for that adapter integration;
selected source authority, root schema decoding, payload digest acceptance and
session effects remain independently unimplemented.

An opt-in sorted Import preflight now shares the existing path-purpose,
encoding, budget and typed-manifest gates while avoiding additional inventory
membership-set allocations on success. It rechecks ZIP-order claims, returns
typed order/count errors and seals the same inventory as general preflight.
An 8,192-case sorted mutation corpus and a 65,534-name ZIP32 boundary guard
acceptance equivalence; the real CRC-checked ZIP fixture checks both paths.
No typed manifest is constructed from root bytes, and no file is imported.

A read-only CRC-correct ZIP regression now exercises sorted Import preflight
through complete entry SHA-256 checks, preserving exact borrowed source bytes.
An altered member with a valid new CRC and unchanged inventory still passes
structural preflight but fails declared SHA-256, even though root syntax and
its exact-source fingerprint remain unchanged. No profile is accepted.

An exhaustive 128-case comparison also proves that both Import preflight
variants return identical earlier path-purpose, archive-encoding and budget
results across all first-release ZIP metadata combinations. Only the final
inventory gate differs by explicit ordering and count diagnostics.

The CRC-checked stored ZIP reader now projects its own archive encoding facts
for Import preflight, replacing synthetic encoding values in integration tests.
This observation is sourced from checked bytes, including legacy ZIP headers,
but still has no filesystem path authority or typed manifest-root provenance.

The pure stored-ZIP encoder can now fill already caller-owned, explicitly
bounded memory without allocating another archive-sized output vector. Exact
size preflight occurs before any mutation, with atomic too-small refusal and
unchanged surplus bytes. This remains byte-only framing, not a canonical
manifest serializer, authorized Export target or atomic filesystem commit.

The read-only Export-byte/Import-byte boundary test now connects caller-owned
ZIP writer output to CRC-checked reading, source-backed encoding observations,
bounded borrowed path views, sorted typed inventory and complete SHA-256 entry
verification. A ten-case matrix additionally proves that both writers and
planning refuse the same malformed or over-budget inputs before output writes.

A new strict typed manifest JSON decoder consumes only already syntax-admitted
root evidence. It enforces exact required object fields, lowercase SHA-256 hex,
plain `u64` lengths, and caller-chosen string, feature and entry ceilings;
unknown wire keys refuse instead of disappearing. A real ZIP fixture now
composes source-backed root decoding with sorted Import preflight and full
member digest verification, including a CRC-correct same-length mutation.
This advances read-only prerequisites but does not open a selected file,
authorize Import or Export, decode handwriting sections or accept a profile.
